     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order because the dose looks unsafe.\n\n- **Dose:** Metformin 1700 mg three times daily comes to 5,100 mg/day. The usual maximum is 2,550 mg/day for immediate-release, and 2,000 mg/day in some labeling. This order is about twice that.\n- **Single dose:** A 1700 mg dose is also well above the usual 500\u20131000 mg per administration.\n- **Risk:** Doses this high raise the risk of lactic acidosis and severe GI toxicity, even with normal kidney and liver function.\n\nMedication orders also need to come from a licensed prescriber, and you've said you're not a clinician. The treating prescriber should confirm the order.\n\nThe dose may be a typo. For example, it might be meant as 850 mg three times daily (2,550 mg/day) or 1000 mg twice daily. If the prescriber confirms the intended dose, I can enter it right away. If they do intend 1700 mg three times daily, they should give me a clinical justification, and I'd suggest a pharmacist review before I enter it."
      }
     ],
